      "math_academy_id": "q-256466",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=-1.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1.2)^{3}+3(-1.2)^{2}+2}{3(-1.2)^{2}+6(-1.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.864}{(-2.88)} \\\\  & ≈-0.994,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
